pcbnew: size the specctra via-name buffer for its widest output

"Via[%d-%d]_%.6g:%.6g_um" can produce 60 characters -- two ints at 11,
two %.6g at 13, 11 of literal text and the terminator -- but the buffer
held 48, so GCC reports the last directive as possibly truncated
(-Wformat-truncation). Truncation here is not cosmetic: the string
becomes the padstack id, so a truncated one silently collides with
another via's.

Reaching the maximum needs implausible layer numbers, which is why this
has never been observed; the buffer is sized to make it unreachable
rather than unlikely.

diff --git a/pcbnew/specctra_import_export/specctra_export.cpp b/pcbnew/specctra_import_export/specctra_export.cpp
index 6cabcbad4b..af260f9d7a 100644
--- a/pcbnew/specctra_import_export/specctra_export.cpp
+++ b/pcbnew/specctra_import_export/specctra_export.cpp
@@ -967,7 +967,11 @@ IMAGE* SPECCTRA_DB::makeIMAGE( BOARD* aBoard, FOOTPRINT* aFootprint )
 PADSTACK* SPECCTRA_DB::makeVia( int aCopperDiameter, int aDrillDiameter,
                                 int aTopLayer, int aBotLayer )
 {
-    char        name[48];
+    // Sized for the widest "Via[%d-%d]_%.6g:%.6g_um" this can produce: two
+    // ints at 11 chars each, two %.6g at 13, 11 of literal text and the
+    // terminator. Truncating instead would silently yield a padstack id that
+    // collides with another via's.
+    char        name[64];
     PADSTACK*   padstack = new PADSTACK();
     double      dsnDiameter = scale( aCopperDiameter );
 
